      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T18:39:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When can the same event be completed multiple times?",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e9e8035c_f307fb74",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 21,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-18T20:22:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The same event isn\u0027t completed multiple times, but a thread checking if it\u0027s done can race with a thread that is completing it. This was seen in https://g-issues.skia.org/issues/418165343 where a race on a thread setting the future ready could race against a `WaitAny` call on that event. I\u0027m pretty sure in that scenario, nothing bad happens, `WaitAny` would just try to wait on a completed serial and do nothing, but making it an atomic got rid of the TSAN issue. Maybe there\u0027s a better way to solve that?",
      "parentUuid": "73d49b33_9410e01c",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0c0ed849_5311ef86",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-13T04:57:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I reverted a decent amount of changes, and decided to remove the buffer lock and continue using the device-wide lock for now. I realized to get it right in the case of a submit, I would need to acquire all the locks of all the buffers, and I would also need to acquire them in a consistent order so it was too much for now. I think probably, the \"correct\" way is to have the lock on the queue instead, but deferring that change for now. There\u0027s some follow up changes I will be doing to clean up some cases where we may re-entrantly acquire the lock, but those cases are invalid uses right now anyways so wanted to unblock this first. Please take another look at everything, thanks!",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f0b70e67_c502b0bf",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-13T19:22:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Just a thought since I was the one originally added the device lock to dawn.\n\nHow about we change the device lock to a recursive mutex? To make everything simpler. I understand recursive mutexes are not recommended in general but the device lock was only an interim solution for multithreading support in Dawn. We want to do a more fine-grained locking approach. Over time we added more locks to the code base to realize that plan. However, they often broke the device lock. Even though the two approaches (device lock vs fine-grained lock) should be independent, it\u0027s very hard to ensure that right now.\n\nIf we used recursive mutex for the device lock we would continue working on the fine-grained locking plan without worrying about breaking/causing dead locks with the device lock. Once that plan is complete, we can remove the device lock entirely.\n\nAs a plus point, recursive mutex\u0027s implementation is pretty efficient nowadays afaik. Microsoft\u0027s D3D11\u0027s multithreading implementation also uses a recursive mutex iiuc.",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d6029e17_d547f99b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-13T20:41:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d be happy to consider that. I agree entirely that it\u0027s been quite tricky to get things right with both the device lock and the smaller locks that lead to unexpected behaviors and lock inversion issues. I think it would especially help a lot with re-entrancy for callbacks atm. The downside would be that it could result in even MORE places using the device lock to synchronize stuff. I feel like one of the main issues with the device lock right now is that it is very hard to tell when and where it NEEDS to be held, and where it\u0027s being held as a byproduct.\n\nAnother thing is that I think if we decide to go the route of making the device lock recursive, we would want to make sure that we remove the `\"no autolock\": true` option entirely so that anytime we call into the API we take the lock. That would probably solve all the issues related to lock-inversion since the device lock will always be held first, but then we would probably take a perf hit for users of the implicit synchronization.",
      "parentUuid": "f0b70e67_c502b0bf",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7640b1c2_84b92a02",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-13T21:16:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027d want to get cwallez@chromium.org thoughts on this also. I was working on a separate change to circumvent some of the issues with the device lock, but depending on the decision here, I might be able to do it differently. \n\nFor context, the separate change was to effectively add a `Defer` option similar to https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/dawn/src/dawn/common/MutexProtected.h;l\u003d152;bpv\u003d0;bpt\u003d0 for the device lock. Then I was going to plumb that through to places that need to execute code without the lock, i.e. places that trigger callbacks. However, if we decide to allow the lock to be recursive, I think I don\u0027t need to do that since this is only an issue for re-entrant spontaneous callbacks, in which case we could just acquire the lock again and it would be fine since it\u0027s recursive.",
      "parentUuid": "d6029e17_d547f99b",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "51028ac9_8f522417",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1003501
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-14T18:03:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Another thing is that I think if we decide to go the route of making the device lock recursive, we would want to make sure that we remove the \"no autolock\": true option entirely so that anytime we call into the API we take the lock.\n\nYou are right that the recursive mutex won\u0027t help resolving locking ordering issues. Though I would prefer we don\u0027t switch to auto lock device on every method. There are certain methods we still want to be lock-free such as command encoding. Locking them would regress the perf too much. Ideally when switching to recursive mutex, it should not impact the perf too much. If we had to reduce perf in order to make it work, I would rather stick to the current implementation then.",
      "parentUuid": "7640b1c2_84b92a02",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0ff40144_2bd6d2a4",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000002
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-17T18:39:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The biggest issue is callback reentrancy, and we should know the complete list of where that is happening + using Loko\u0027s UseWithDefer we should be able to handle it nicely. The other one is when reentrancy happens inside Dawn code that doesn\u0027t know if the incoming code path locked the device mutex or not. It seems that 90%+ of it is from workarounds that need to allocate during command encoding, which we want to fix eventually.\n\nIt seems that it might be worth going the recursive mutex route, but I really want us to eventually have only fine-grained locking, and the pain of using the non-recursive device mutex helps remind us of that. How much pain would be saved right now by using a recursive one?",
      "parentUuid": "51028ac9_8f522417",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9df1faa5_cf105a5b",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-18T21:53:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think one area that the recursive mutex would help a lot with in the current state is error handling. Whenever an error can occur on the device right now (and this is practically everywhere...), we either need to ensure we take the device lock before calling `ConsumedError`, or there is an assumption that the upper layer (i.e. generated code), already has the lock.\n\nWhat the recursive lock would allow us to do there is just take the lock in the `Device::ConsumeError` implementation no matter what. From what I can tell, that alone might allow us to take the lock in a lot less APIs since in a lot of them in some backends, we just need it in case we generate an error (which ideally isn\u0027t the common path).",
      "parentUuid": "0ff40144_2bd6d2a4",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e2013555_3b1dda70",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 29
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000014
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-06-19T01:20:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t have any better idea than anyone else here about whether a recursive mutex would save pain in general. It does sound like it would help for ConsumeError cases. However I am not sure if it would have helped with the problem in this CL - in the earlier version we tried to introduced a finer-grained lock for the buffer state struct, but getting this correct while a device lock also exists was incredibly hard, hence switching to protecting only the buffer state enum with an atomic.",
      "parentUuid": "9df1faa5_cf105a5b",
      "revId": "67d5a0852c60a7c2c02a8a2aaf34b3ce4fd2de4e",
      "serverId": "dd02978d-1a8e-36d7-bcc0-a5723e5c0abd"
